// `$!!` is an early return, not a throw.
//
// `GUIDE.md` § "Value flow" states it: «`$!!` … does not throw an exception, so
// it cannot be caught with `!?/:!`». The register VM and the browser engine
// followed the rule; the tree-walker did not — it looked inside a pending
// return, and if the value there was an error it handed it to the catch clause.
//
// So a function propagating a failure upwards was intercepted by its own catch
// clause whenever the `$!!` happened to sit inside a `!?`. The two flows the
// language keeps apart on purpose — exceptions through `!?/:!`, values through
// `$!`/`$!!` — met in the one place they must not.
//
// Found while writing a test for BUG-ZYB-007, not by looking for it: the
// natural shape of "catch a database failure" put a `$!!` inside a `!?`, and
// the three engines gave two different answers.
riesgoso(i) {
    !? { <~ [10, 20][i] } :! { <~ _err }
}

// `$!!` inside `!?`: returns from `propaga`, ignoring the catch clause.
propaga(i) {
    !? {
        e = riesgoso(i)
        ? e$! { e$!! }
        <~ "sin error"
    } :! {
        <~ "CAPTURADO — no debería"
    }
}

>> "hay elemento: " propaga(1) ¶
>> "no lo hay:    " propaga(99) ¶

// An ordinary `<~` of an error value is value flow too, and equally uncaught.
devuelve() {
    !? {
        <~ riesgoso(99)
    } :! {
        <~ "CAPTURADO — no debería"
    }
}
>> "devuelto:     " devuelve() ¶

// What `:!` IS for: an exception raised inside the block.
lanza() {
    !? {
        <~ [1, 2][99]
    } :! {
        <~ "capturado, y así debe ser"
    }
}
>> "lanzado:      " lanza() ¶

// A finally clause beside a returning try block is its own case, next door in
// finally_con_retorno.zy — it was found while verifying this one, and the two
// fixes are independent.
